  "notes": "An MPC model, and the research makes swing a stated number rather than a feel: \"I chopped it up on the MPC and the beat had a '21 Questions' type of swing\", with 16ths pushed roughly 8–15%. That lands `session.swing.amount` at 0.57 on the 16th grid, the only swung entry in this batch and the parameter a producer would notice first. The second signature is the hat — \"our hi-hat sample ... which became synonymous with our sound\", a popping Miami-bass-derived hat rather than a stock 808 one — so the hats are `continuous` 16ths with an unusually hot main velocity band and a hard accent split, where crunk's hats stop and start at a flat level. Harmony is inherited from the source records rather than invented: ii–V and i–iv motion over 2–4 bar chopped loops, sevenths carrying nearly half the extension weight, and horn and guitar stabs \"cut for punch\", which is what the short `chordDurationBeats` floor and the high melodic density are. ⛔ `drums.bass808.role` moves off `bassline` because the research says a live bass player was brought in over the sample with the 808 only reinforcing underneath; the `bassline` block is that player. `counter_riff` is the engine's only other role and is a near-fit, held still by a near-zero `slideProb`. ⚠ The half-time option the research allows is NOT modelled: this entry commits to the 76–92 straight lane. The tempo band itself is marked LOW-CONF in the research and is the one INFERRED number here; the MPC workflow, the swing character, the hat signature and the live-bass habit are all directly sourced. ⚠ `fills.snareRollLadder` is off — with both fill periods at 4 bars the big fill wins and the inherited ladder reads no seed, writing one identical fill on all 200. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 49 to 48 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 49 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 1.",
